# LazyMotion

`LazyMotion` pairs with the `m` namespace to choose which runtime features are active for a subtree, like Motion's `LazyMotion`. `m` components get the features of the nearest `LazyMotion` bundle. `motion` components always have every feature and ignore `LazyMotion`. An `m` outside `LazyMotion` renders its `style` and `initial` and does nothing else.

In svelte-motion, `LazyMotion` selects behaviour but does not yet reduce bundle size: every feature's code is bundled either way.

```svelte
<script>
    import { LazyMotion, domAnimation, m } from '@humanspeak/svelte-motion'
</script>

<LazyMotion features={domAnimation}>
    <m.div
        initial={{ opacity: 0, y: 20 }}
        animate={{ opacity: 1, y: 0 }}
        whileHover={{ scale: 1.05 }}
    />
</LazyMotion>
```

## Feature bundles

| Bundle | Includes |
|---|---|
| `domMin` | mount/update animations |
| `domAnimation` | animations plus hover, tap, focus, pan, and in-view gestures |
| `domMax` | everything in `domAnimation` plus drag and layout animations |

Use `domAnimation` for most interface motion. Use `domMin` for pages that only need entrance/update animation. Use `domMax` when the subtree uses `drag`, `layout`, or `layoutId`.

## Async loading

`features` can also be a function returning a promise. Until the bundle resolves, the subtree's `m` components render their `initial` state and do nothing else. When it resolves they animate in. If the loader rejects, `m` components stay as they were rendered.

```svelte
<script>
    import { LazyMotion, domAnimation, m } from '@humanspeak/svelte-motion'

    const loadFeatures = async () => domAnimation
</script>

<LazyMotion features={loadFeatures}>
    <m.button whileHover={{ scale: 1.08 }}>Hover me</m.button>
</LazyMotion>
```

## Without LazyMotion

An `m` with no `LazyMotion` above it has no animation runtime, as in Motion. It renders its `style`, `initial`, `tabindex` and drag styles, and runs no animation, gesture, drag, layout or exit animation. Inside `AnimatePresence` it is removed at once.

```svelte
<!-- Renders at opacity 0.5 and stays there. -->
<m.div initial={{ opacity: 0.5 }} animate={{ opacity: 1 }} />
```

## Strict mode

Pass `strict` to make sure a subtree only uses `m` components. In development, rendering a `motion` component inside `<LazyMotion strict>` throws:

```text
You have rendered a `motion` component within a `LazyMotion` component. This will break tree shaking. Import and render a `m` component instead.
```

Production builds skip the check.

The main `Reorder` renders `motion` components too, but you render `Reorder`, not `motion`. Inside `<LazyMotion strict>` it warns once in development instead of throwing, and the warning names the fix: import `Reorder` from `@humanspeak/svelte-motion/m`.

## Reorder with LazyMotion

`Reorder` from the `@humanspeak/svelte-motion/m` entry renders `m` components, so it gets its features from `LazyMotion`. Reordering needs drag and layout, so give it `domMax`. Without a `LazyMotion` that provides `domMax`, this `Reorder` does nothing.

```svelte
<script>
    import { LazyMotion, domMax } from '@humanspeak/svelte-motion'
    import { Reorder } from '@humanspeak/svelte-motion/m'

    let items = $state([0, 1, 2, 3])
</script>

<LazyMotion features={domMax} strict>
    <Reorder.Group axis="y" values={items} onReorder={(next) => (items = next)}>
        {#each items as item (item)}
            <Reorder.Item value={item}>{item}</Reorder.Item>
        {/each}
    </Reorder.Group>
</LazyMotion>
```

The `@humanspeak/svelte-motion/m` entry also exports `m`.

